A||||14||Tom88gt||88 v-6 won't start.  just sputters.||||9||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||12-20-1999||02:12 PM||||I just bought an 88 Formula.  It it wouldn't start but the rest of the car looked good and the price was right.  Here is what I know;<P>1. the engine turns over<BR>2. it has fuel<BR>3. when I pushed in the fuel pressure relief valve on the fuel injection fuel shot out<BR>4. When I turn the key on it sounds like the pump is running for a couple of seconds, but very quietly<BR>5. The engine fires but won't run, sometimes fires a few times<BR>6. Code 12 (normal) with a code reader<BR>7. air filter is good, but not new<P>I've got fuel, heat & air.  So why no fire?<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-20-1999||02:43 PM||||What about spark?||||reg||1
Z||000002||HF6582||12-20-1999||02:51 PM||||You say that it has fire, but does it look weak. <P>Also check fuel pressure, maybe not enough pressure (weak pump or clogged fuel filter).<P>Before you change everything under the sun just make sure the basics are where they should be.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||12-21-1999||11:44 AM||||replaced cap and rotor.  sputters more but still no start.  fuel pressure strong.  The cap hand lots of green corrosion on the copper terminals. the "spider" looking thing??? below the rotor and above the ignition pick-up is rusty.  Was told by a local v-6 owner to check the ignition module.  it is not covered by a check engine code?||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-21-1999||01:20 PM||||Sounds like the module to me too.  These cars (V-6) are infamous for modules dying.  It is also a good idea to keep an extra one of these in the car (AND ONLY USE GM MODULES... unless going to high performance).  The module is not equipt with any form of sensor, so the OBD will not help you when it comes to spark.  While you are at it, consider replacing the wires too.  AC-Delco parts are the most reliable (unless stepping up to high performance) and usually have a life span of around 2-3 yrs.  <P>Good Luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000005||88 Database Nut||12-21-1999||02:04 PM||||Hey, congrats on the new car.  Just a thought, have you verified all the fuses are good?  Maybe the injectors aren't firing, and if the battery was disconnected by the dealer, all the codes would be gone until it runs again, right?<BR>Just a shot in the dark, I'd bet on the pickup coil or ig. module with the others.<BR>Good Luck!||||reg||5
Z||000006||Tom88gt||12-21-1999||04:32 PM||||The module checked good, Autozone test bench.  The wires are pretty new, 80K on the receipt.  Going to pull the dist and check/replace the ignition pick-up.  I'm sure the battery has been dead at least once since the problem started.  But wouldn't trying to start it, or even just power applied allow the OBD to work?||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-21-1999||05:23 PM||||You may have spark but it may not be strong enough. The tach filter could be going bad. When these go out, I believe the coil isn't able to produce a strong spark anymore, or no spark. You could try disconnecting the filter, try to start the engine, and if it starts, then you know you need a new tach filter. The tach will remain stationary since it is disconnected, but no harm will be done to anything.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||12-21-1999||05:25 PM||||Forgot to mention, the tach filter is a little can located just ahead of the distributor.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||12-22-1999||08:47 AM||||It's alive!!!  It was the plugs, for the most part.  I only pulled one at first.  A little sooty but looked ok so I looked elsewhere.  A couple were fouled with oil.  Replaced the plugs and it fired right up.  I replaced the distributor anyway, $94 at Autozone with lifetime warranty.  Everything checked good but the cap, rotor, module, pick-up were badly coroded and I'm sure compounded the problem and would have gone out completely very soon.  Engine runs smooth & strong!||||reg||10
Z||000010||88 Database Nut||12-22-1999||09:51 AM||||Hey, Congrats on bringing abother one back to life.  Glad you figured it out.  I think that<BR>$94 bucks you spent on a distributor will pay off handsomely in savings from breakdowns later on.  Since things are slow with my '88 database project, I have to ask if you'ld mind giving me some basic info on your new Formula?  I am trying to start a registry of the '88s, and have 885 cars now.  All I'd need is the VIN and short (or long if you want) description of the car (please include factory colors).  If you don't want your name associated that's 100% ok, and I won't add it to the database.<BR>About the codes, since it takes awhile for the ECM to 'relearn' after the battery is disconnected, I don't think the OBD system will work without a breakin period.  I could be wrong though, does anybody have some thoughts about this?  Sounds like something which would be good to KNOW.<BR>Happy Holidays,<BR>John aka 88 nut||||reg||7
Z||000011||Tom88gt||12-22-1999||12:32 PM||||Here's some info off the top of my head, don't have the vin in front of me.<P>88 Formula, C&C t-tops, white, grey interior, 5sp, paid $1200, runs strong, everything works (cruise, windows, locks, radio).<P>Hope this helps your database.||||reg||1
